(* README *)

1 - Summary
    -------

    M001 formalizes CONSTRUCTIVE REGULATOR THEORY: a certified checker for
    finite proofs in the closed implication/falsity language

      A, B ::= Bot | Imp A B.

    The checker, proof transformations, and symbolic release layer are tied
    together by two exact correspondences:

      - `regulator_theory_syntactic_adequacy_lemma`
          checked derivability <-> inductive derivability

      - `regulator_theory_symbolic_regulator_release_iff`
          checked derivability <-> release by some finite proof instruction


2 - Reading Contract
    ----------------

    M001 verifies supplied finite proof scripts; it does not search for proofs.
    A regulator theory contains a logical profile and a Boolean axiom-membership
    function. A context supplies temporary assumptions and is not part of the
    theory.

    `Formula` has only `Bot` and `Imp`. Object-language negation is the
    abbreviation

      formula_negation A = Imp A Bot.

    `regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma p A = true` means that every line of
    `p` checks in `R` under `Gamma` and that the final line is exactly `A`.
    Acceptance establishes syntactic derivability, not semantic truth in an
    external model.

    `symbolic_regulator_release S i b` is a pure Boolean gate. It returns
    `Some b` exactly when instruction `i` is accepted for output `b`, and
    returns `None` otherwise. It does not synthesize `b`, perform side effects,
    or provide an actuator, environment, dynamics, quotation, self-application,
    or fixed-point principle. Applications interpret or execute released
    outputs.

    `AxiomSet` is a caller-supplied Boolean function and need not have a finite
    enumeration. `FiniteAxiomSet` is the separate literal-list interface for
    clients whose theory description must itself be finite data.


3 - State
    -----

                      ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
                      │                x                 │
                      │   └─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┴─┘ 7 / 10   │
                      └──────────────────────────────────┘

    Evaluation: 7, Serviceable. Mechanized, documented, and extracted. The
    checker and transformations are constructive. The release layer is small
    and intentionally pure. Not peer reviewed.


4 - Intuition
    ---------

    A proof is a finite list of formulas annotated by one of three
    justifications:

      Assumption:  A occurs in Gamma
      Axiom:       A is K, S, optional EFQ, or accepted by the axiom source
      MP i j:      earlier lines contain C and C -> A, in either reference order

    The checker reads the script from left to right. MP references are resolved
    only against the already checked prefix, and the last formula must equal the
    requested output.

      R, Gamma, proof p, formula A

                    │
                    ▼

      regulator_theory_check_bool ── true ──▶ checked derivation of A

    Checked derivability forgets the concrete script existentially. The
    independent inductive judgment has assumption, axiom, and MP constructors,
    and syntactic adequacy proves the two presentations equivalent:

      regulator_theory_checked_derivable R Gamma A
        <->
      regulator_theory_inductive_derivable R Gamma A.

    The canonical proof regulator packages the same machine through the generic
    symbolic-regulator interface:

      instruction = Proof
      output      = Formula
      acceptance  = regulator_theory_check_bool R Gamma.

    The release gate converts its Boolean verdict into an output protocol:

      proof p, output A
             │
             ▼
      symbolic_regulator_release
             │
             ├── accepted ──▶ Some A
             `── rejected ──▶ None

    Therefore a formula is checked derivable exactly when some finite proof
    instruction releases it. A listener may consume `Some A`, but that listener
    and any external behavior remain outside M001.

    Three verified transformations operate on accepted scripts. MP composition
    joins proofs of `A -> B` and `A`; deduction compiles a proof of `B` under
    `A :: Gamma` into a proof of `A -> B` under `Gamma`; reductio specializes
    deduction to `B = Bot`. The last operation is constructive negation
    introduction, not classical reductio or double-negation elimination.


